   {:report
    "Comparison Report: microFIT Contract Version 2.0 (file: microFIT-Contract-Version-2-0.pdf) vs Version 3.1.1 (file: microFIT-Contract-Version-3-1-1.pdf)\n\nExecutive summary\n- Version 3.1.1 is an update that (1) introduces a formal \"Prescribed Form\" requirement for multiple supplier communications and contract formalities, (2) removes or substantially reduces the Domestic Content obligations (Appendix C and related references), and (3) clarifies/expands certain information disclosure uses (e.g., public health & safety / emergency planning). There are also several minor editorial and definitional changes (new definitions and a Website reference).\n- The most material changes for suppliers are: removal of explicit domestic content tables/verification requirements and the new requirement to use the Prescribed Form for notices, amendments, contact updates and termination notices.\n\nImplications (high level)\n- Domestic content: Version 3.1.1 omits the Domestic Content Requirements (the Domestic Content Grids and related obligations/verification language). If your compliance, procurement, or record-keeping depended on the domestic-content tables in v2.0, those obligations appear removed (or moved elsewhere) in v3.1.1 — this reduces supplier obligations tied to domestic content but also removes OPA's explicit mechanism for calculating/confirming Domestic Content Level in the Contract text.\n- Formality/Administration: Requiring the \"Prescribed Form\" for notice, amendments, contact changes and termination increases formality and could limit informal communications being treated as valid. Suppliers should ensure they can access and use the OPA \"Prescribed Form\" and Website for submissions/notifications.\n- Information sharing: Version 3.1.1 expands/discusses disclosure to other government ministries for public health & safety and emergency planning; suppliers should expect potentially broader information sharing for those purposes.\n- No substantive changes found to pricing/indexing mechanism (Appendix B formula retained), to core guarantees (LDC failure to pay), assignment rules, dispute resolution or the core structure of the contract beyond those administrative and domestic-content changes.\n\nScope / methodology\n- Compared the two supplied contract texts section-by-section and noted additions, removals and edits that change obligations or clarify administrative procedures. Focused on differences likely to affect supplier compliance, administrative burden, and commercial/legal rights.\n\nKey actionable items for a supplier reviewing v3.1.1\n- Confirm whether domestic content obligations still apply via other program rules (microFIT Rules, OPA website) or whether they have been removed entirely — do not rely solely on the contract text if you have outstanding compliance expectations.\n- Obtain and review the \"Prescribed Form\" as published on the OPA Website and incorporate it into internal notification & change-control processes.\n- Update privacy/data-sharing expectations and confirm what information the OPA may disclose for public health & safety / emergency planning.\n- Ensure administrative staff can access the OPA Website referenced and store copies of any Prescribed Form submissions and confirmations.",
